#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148386} :activity/id #uuid "e3ff63f4-b713-5986-8c6d-ad2b66c650aa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741 :topic/id #uuid "10441679-94f4-5ae1-8bff-8e5945144d9c" :topic/math-academy-id 2532} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148769 :tutorial/content "The **phase line** of an autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nis a vertical $y$-axis marked with equilibrium values and arrows indicating whether solutions increase or decrease in each interval.\n\nSince the rate of change depends only on $y$ (and not on $x$), we can sketch a one-dimensional diagram that describes how the system behaves in different intervals of $y.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example and construct the phase line for the autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = (y+1)(y-1).\n$$\n\nTo draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y) < 0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | ${\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ |\n| $y-1$ | ${\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{-}}$ | ${\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty, -1) \\cup (1, \\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) < 0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/98/9861bea513671d39d85e1f0bafc975118613daeb7dd399e1cf9108a078062f4b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "228d941e-82e9-5159-b563-975a96888cd6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5569 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d34ff0d2-946a-52ae-a32a-d005e7eb06c4" :step/math-academy-id 593578 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147490 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147384 :question/id #uuid "adebd9bd-412d-5e0e-ab21-0087a5c35d9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395350}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"} :step/id #uuid "43a23b93-83e7-52e1-89aa-90625941dc2e" :step/math-academy-id 593579 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147491 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147382 :question/id #uuid "842d6fb8-d895-584f-9a49-5d90409f1ba2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8533"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051068}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395528}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "5e6a17ba-d700-510e-b2d5-945ee4fa25e5" :step/math-academy-id 593582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148439}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147388 :question/id #uuid "fba1d686-de7e-5e8e-ae0b-1f4f04fa79ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8586"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368955}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"} :step/id #uuid "4de9a841-9218-5356-bf72-17107cf67bd9" :step/math-academy-id 593585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148387}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148770 :tutorial/content "Below is the slope field for the autonomous differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = (y+1)(y-1).$\n\n![](images/a5/a51eadb566c1d01ea3973914decec266e7253797c0308e7a6c0a2ddf3baa5bbd.png)\n\nNotice that for autonomous differential equations, the slope marks are invariant horizontally; that is, any two points with the same $y$-coordinate have the same slope.\n\nTherefore, drawing more than one vertical line of slope marks is redundant, since each vertical line is identical.\n\nInstead of drawing the full slope field, we can represent all the necessary information on a single vertical line. This is the **phase line**.\n\nThe diagram below shows how the slope field for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\underbrace{(y+1)(y-1)}_{\\large f(y)}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to its phase line.\n\n![](images/4c/4cc5ca68d8e7588b809c0e87b24915669fb0d62c333a9ea75d62098f097de972.png)\n\nThe direction of the arrows on the phase line is determined by the sign of $f(y)$:\n\n- If $f(y) > 0$ on an interval, the slopes are positive ($\\dfrac{dy}{dx} > 0$), and solutions $y(x)$ increase. The arrow on the phase line points **up**.\n\n- If $f(y) < 0$ on an interval, the slopes are negative ($\\dfrac{dy}{dx} < 0$), and solutions $y(x)$ decrease. The arrow on the phase line points **down**." :tutorial/id #uuid "fc44d777-d50e-5f1a-85d7-d7994ec043fc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5570 :tutorial/title "Connection Between Phase Lines and Slope Fields"} :step/id #uuid "287daa69-be4c-5b66-8276-bbc42b6247a1" :step/math-academy-id 593588 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147493 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147383 :question/id #uuid "45254b75-3db0-5c50-970a-4eea7db883f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8534"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c72cc6e0-deae-51fc-a5e4-b27fcb6f7892" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395410}] :knowledge-point/title "Phase Lines and Slope Fields"} :step/id #uuid "6a27dd7b-c25f-53bc-bd43-6ca382e43421" :step/math-academy-id 593589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148439 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148669 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we know that the autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nhas the following phase line:\n\n![](images/83/83d1a80bef714ffe5806d0247c7f5c1aebe0b5c2aeb8270baf730b190080446a.png)\n\nWhat does this phase line tell us about the function $f(y)$?\n\nWe can interpret the key features of the phase line as follows:\n\n- The solid dots are the **equilibrium solutions**, where \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y) = 0.\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2$.\n\n- The arrows show the sign of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y)$ on the intervals between the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty, -1),$ the arrow points down, so the derivative $f(y)$ is *negative*.\n\n- On the interval $(-1, 2),$ the arrow points up, so the derivative $f(y)$ is *positive*.\n\n- On the interval $(2, \\infty),$ the arrow points up, so the derivative $f(y)$ is *positive*.\n\nThe graph of a *possible* derivative function $f(y)$ looks as follows.\n\n![](images/06/06e2c08b823a49d069e4eaa23b8642eb8c17f08fea437ebf213abc6fe737134d.png)\n\nOn the next slide, we will see how we can use the given phase line to identify the corresponding differential equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7520b35-b0a6-5676-898b-1b8dd7163a6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14933 :tutorial/title "Analyzing Properties of a Differential Equation Given Its Phase Line"} :step/id #uuid "485ce960-e9f1-5aa4-8ec8-b8aa608d874b" :step/math-academy-id 610176 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148389}}] :activity/title "Phase Lines of First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147711}]}
